<?nvs
// Running another program, as a program: an argv, a result, and the target
// this language will not run at all.
//
// [ADR 0044](/docs/decisions/0044.md) is the
// whole of it. `Core\Process::run` takes a path and an `array<string>`, and
// there is no shell-string form *anywhere* in the surface — nothing to
// escape, because there is nothing to escape into. A tainted `$path` or
// `$argv` element is a compile-time diagnostic, which is why this fixture
// cannot demonstrate that half: a program that tried would not compile.
//
// What it can demonstrate is the runtime half of the same rule. A Windows
// batch or PowerShell target is refused by the API, because `cmd.exe`
// re-parses its own arguments after we have finished quoting them — an
// argv Novis built correctly is a shell string again by the time the batch
// file sees it. The refusal is by target kind and not by content, so
// `examples/process/say.bat` exists on disk: the third line below is
// evidence about what the target *is*, not about whether it is there.
//
// The child is this repository's own `nvs`, which the loop driver has
// already built — `Core\IO::exists` picks the platform's spelling rather
// than a `Core\Env::OS` comparison, so the fixture reads the same on both
// legs.
//
// The three lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

string $nvs = 'target/debug/nvs';
if (Core\IO::exists('target/debug/nvs.exe')) {
    $nvs = 'target/debug/nvs.exe';
}

// argv only: the subcommand and the script are elements, never a line.
//
// The captured streams are `bytes` and not `string` (ADR 0044 § 1): a child
// process makes no UTF-8 promise, so `as string` is the checked conversion
// that throws rather than a silent mangling.
var $result = Core\Process::run($nvs, ['run', 'examples/process/hello.nvs']);
echo 'exit=', $result->exitCode(), "\n";
echo 'stdout=', Core\Str::trim($result->stdout() as string), "\n";

// The target kind this API declines to run. The clause is `RuntimeError`
// and not `Throwable`, so a missing file or a capability denial would not
// have been quietly counted as this refusal.
try {
    Core\Process::run('examples/process/say.bat', []);
    echo 'accepted: a batch target', "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo 'refused: no shell form', "\n";
}
